\subsection{Classical-logic core}
\label{sec:p01-classical-symbols}

The classical-logic core is the set of global-scope rows of the register.
Table~\ref{tab:p01-classical-symbols} prints exactly those rows from
\texttt{registry/symbols.csv}; no second, hand-maintained extract is kept,
so a change to a core row changes this lookup table when the tables are
regenerated.

\section{Binding and precedence}
\label{sec:p01-binding-precedence}
\index{precedence}\index{binding}

\begin{hbconvention}{def:p01-w02-p01-026-precedence}{Binding and precedence}
The grammar declared by the active language is always primary.  After unique
readability for that language has been established, the numerical rank
recorded in an operator's register row licenses omitted parentheses in
expository formulas:
\begin{enumerate}
\item a smaller rank binds more tightly;
\item rank 1 (function and predicate application, and unary term operations
  such as a successor or complement sign) binds tightest, and its arguments
  are exactly those fixed by the symbol's arity;
\item rank 2 operators (declared atomic relations such as \(=,\leq,>\)) are
  nonassociative;
\item ranks 2.5 and 3 are unary formula operators; each governs the
  immediately adjacent formula (the following one for a prefix sign such as
  a quantifier or \(\neg\), the preceding one for a postfix sign such as
  \(A^\perp\)) unless parentheses extend its scope;
\item rank 3.5 (binary temporal operators such as until) is nonassociative;
\item ranks 4 and 5 (conjunction-level and disjunction-level operators) are
  left associative;
\item rank 6 (implication-level operators) is right associative;
\item rank 7 (the biconditional) is nonassociative, so repeated
  biconditionals require parentheses;
\item the signs \(\defeq,\Leftrightarrow,\entails,\derives\) and their
  declared subscripted forms are metalinguistic, carry the descriptor
  \texttt{meta} instead of a rank, and are never object-language operators.
\end{enumerate}
Two operators share a rank only when their recorded scopes are disjoint or
the operators are declared at the same level of one grammar.  A rank not
listed above is valid only in the scope recorded in its row, whose grammar
fixes its association.
\end{hbconvention}

\begin{hbconvention}{def:p01-w02-p01-026-quantifier-scope}{Quantifier scope}
A quantifier \(\forall x\) or \(\exists x\) is a prefix operator of rank 3.
In an abbreviated display it governs the immediately following formula and
binds more tightly than every binary connective; parentheses are the only
way to extend its scope.  Hence \(\forall x\,P(x)\to Q(x)\) abbreviates
\((\forall x\,P(x))\to Q(x)\), and the implication lies inside the
quantifier's scope only when written \(\forall x\,(P(x)\to Q(x))\).
\end{hbconvention}

Table~\ref{tab:p01-precedence} lists every register row that
carries a numerical rank, ordered by rank and generated from
\texttt{registry/symbols.csv}.  It applies the two conventions above; the
scope column says where each ranked operator is active.

